  <h1>What to Do After an FMLA Violation</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Document the violation immediately</h2><p>Write a detailed account: the FMLA leave request or usage, all communications about the leave, any adverse action taken, and the timeline. Save all emails, HR notices, leave approval/denial letters, and performance reviews. If you received verbal communications, document them promptly in writing.</p>
  <h2>2. Preserve comparator evidence</h2><p>Note whether similarly situated employees who did not take FMLA leave received different treatment. If you were terminated, identify who performed your job duties afterward and their FMLA usage history. This comparator evidence is critical for retaliation claims.</p>
  <h2>3. File with the DOL or sue directly</h2><p>Unlike Title VII and ADEA, FMLA does not require EEOC exhaustion. You can file a complaint with the Department of Labor&rsquo;s Wage and Hour Division (free, but limited to back pay and reinstatement) or file directly in federal court (allows full damages including liquidated damages and attorney fees). Most plaintiffs with significant claims file in federal court for maximum recovery.</p>
  <h2>4. Watch the statute of limitations</h2><p>Two years for ordinary violations; three years for willful violations. The clock typically starts at the date of the adverse action. Do not wait &mdash; evidence fades, witnesses leave the company, and electronic records are purged on retention schedules.</p>
  <h2>5. Consult an employment attorney</h2><p>FMLA retaliation cases turn heavily on timing, comparator evidence, and pretext analysis. An experienced employment attorney will evaluate whether the employer&rsquo;s stated reason for the adverse action is pretextual and advise whether to file with the DOL or directly in court.</p>
